    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my peace lily leaves turning yellow?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yellow peace lily leaves usually come from watering problems. Clemson Extension says soil that dries out too much makes the leaves and edges turn yellow, and that root and stem diseases from overwatering are the most common peace lily problem. Yellow between green veins can point to a magnesium deficiency."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my peace lily leaf tips turning brown?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "NC State Extension lists direct sun, underwatering and low humidity as causes of brown peace lily leaf tips. Too much fertiliser and salt build-up burn the tips as well, and UF/IFAS notes that frequent drooping from thirst leads to brown tips and edges."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my peace lily leaves pale or scorched?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Pale, bleached peace lily leaves are a sign of too much light, and direct sun scorches the leaves. UF/IFAS notes that the pale colour is often mistaken for a nutrient deficiency. In the UAE this often happens near west- or south-facing glass."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my peace lily not flowering?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A peace lily usually does not flower because it is less than a year old or gets too little light (Clemson Extension). Shop plants are often brought into flower by the grower, so they may not flower again until their natural season."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why does my peace lily have black patches after cold air?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Black areas on peace lily leaves that start at the tips and spread inwards, with stunted growth, are a sign of cold damage. UF/IFAS links them to cold drafts and long periods below about 7 °C; older leaves are more sensitive than new ones."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is the white cotton on my peace lily?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "White, cottony clusters on a peace lily are mealybugs, which NC State Extension calls probably the most common insect on peace lilies indoors. Clemson Extension also lists spider mites, scale and fungus gnats as possible pests."
