     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is a peace lily a real lily?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "No. A peace lily (Spathiphyllum) is not a true lily but a member of the arum family, Araceae (Clemson Extension). The white 'flower' is a spathe, a modified leaf around a spike of tiny true flowers."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my peace lily flowers turning green?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Peace lily flowers turn pale green as they age. NC State Extension says the blooms stay white for about 10 days and then stay attractive for at least another month. UF/IFAS advises trimming dead flowers as soon as possible."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Should you cut off old peace lily flowers?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. UF/IFAS advises trimming dead peace lily flowers and leaves as soon as possible. Use clean scissors."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How big does a peace lily get?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Most peace lilies grow 30 cm to 1.2 m tall and wide (Clemson Extension), and indoors they typically reach about 90 cm (NC State Extension). Large cultivars such as 'Sensation' can grow much bigger, and dwarf types stay small."
     }
    },
